# THE NAMED CATCHERS WERE STALE, AND THE GATE DISTINGUISHED THAT FROM A MISSING CONTROL.
# Reported UNATTRIBUTED: the suite went red, but one named catcher did not fail while a test
# this file never named did.
#
# This mutation replaces rule 9b's `if n <> 1 then` with `if false then`, which disables the
# SENTINEL COUNT leg and nothing else. Rule 9b has three branches, and only two of them read
# that count:
#
#   * "a reference sentinel deleted"                      -> counts rows   -> FAILS, correct
#   * "counts the ROW, so re-pointing the registry does
#      not satisfy it"                                    -> counts rows   -> FAILS, correct
#   * "a registry row naming a column that does not
#      exist"                                             -> validates the REGISTRY against
#                                                            pg_attribute, never reaching the
#                                                            count                -> unaffected
#
# So the third control was named in error. It is NOT dropped to make the gate green: the leg
# it guards gets its own mutation, M29, so removing it from this list does not leave that
# branch unproven. Editing a catcher list until the gate passes is the one move that would
# make this whole mechanism worthless.
# M20 — posture rule 9b (REFERENCE SENTINELS) made unmatchable.
#
# THE GAP THIS RULE CLOSED, WHICH IS WHY IT NEEDS ITS OWN CONTROL. The pre-port rule 9
# SKIPPED any table listed in public.posture_exempt_table, and the pre-port migrations/0008
# (that set is deleted; it is in git history at commit b3a5f59) used that to waive its five
# reference tables because their sentinels are keyed by `id`, not `user_id`.
# The sentinels were seeded and the comment argued at length that the evidence requirement
# was met — but NOTHING CHECKED THEM. Five protected tables carried an unenforced sentinel:
# true the day it was written, with nothing to notice when it stopped being true. M8 covers
# rule 9a only, so without this mutation the half of rule 9 that was added to close that
# gap would have no control.
#
# WHY A SENTINEL IS NOW INDISPENSABLE RATHER THAN THOROUGH. `app.require_user_id()` raised
# 28000; `auth.uid()` returns NULL. A claimless read of a protected table therefore returns
# zero rows and raises nothing (MEASURED). On an EMPTY protected table a correct policy, a
# broken policy and no policy at all are three names for the same observation, so the
# seeded row is the only thing that turns a zero back into evidence.
#
# The mutation makes the "registered, so count it" branch unfailable while leaving the
# registry, the identifier checks and the `execute format` all in place — so the rule still
# reads the catalog, still interpolates safely, and still reports nothing.
#
# EXPECTATION UNVERIFIED: the gate could not run in the session that registered this (the
# baseline integration suite is not green). Probes C3 and C4 in docs/supabase-port-notes.md
# §6 record this rule firing by execution for a deleted reference sentinel and for a
# registry row naming a nonexistent column, so `uncaught` here is a backlog marker, not a
# measurement.
id: M20
title: posture rule 9b (reference-table sentinel count) made unfailable
expect: caught
proves: rule 9b enforces the reference-table sentinels the pre-port rule 9 waived (V18, port notes 4)
expect-test: [M20] raises ST099 for a reference sentinel deleted
expect-test: [M20] rule 9b counts the ROW, so re-pointing the registry does not satisfy it

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
        if n <> 1 then
          bad := bad || format(
            E'\n  rule 9b: sentinel rows in %s where %s = %s: %s, expected exactly 1',
@@REPLACE
        if false then
          bad := bad || format(
            E'\n  rule 9b: sentinel rows in %s where %s = %s: %s, expected exactly 1',
@@END
